<% content_for :title, "About" %>

<article class="about">
  <header>
    <h1>About AI Music Tube</h1>
    <p class="about-intro">Discover music made with AI, and see what the technology actually did.</p>
  </header>

  <dl class="about-stats">
    <div><dt>YouTube uploads</dt><dd><%= number_with_delimiter @track_count %></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Artists and channels</dt><dd><%= number_with_delimiter @artist_count %></dd></div>
    <div><dt><%= link_to "Catalog tags", tags_path, class: "text-link" %></dt><dd><%= number_with_delimiter @genre_count + Track::LABELS.size %></dd></div>
  </dl>
  <p class="stats-note">A song, album, or mix counts as one upload.</p>

  <section>
      <h2>Why this catalog exists</h2>
      <p>People talk a lot about how AI is changing software development, but I rarely hear anyone around me talk about AI-generated music or what it means for the music industry. I wanted to show why it’s a bigger deal than many of us realize: even with a trained ear, it’s not always easy to tell whether the music you’re hearing was made by a person or generated by AI.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Know an artist we’re missing?</h2>
    <p><%= link_to "Edit db/catalog.json on GitHub", artist_catalog_edit_url, class: "text-link" %> and open a pull request to suggest an artist. Include a link to their music on YouTube and a source for any AI music credit.</p>
  </section>

  <p>AI Music Tube isn’t affiliated with or endorsed by YouTube or Google.</p>
  <p><%= link_to "Browse the catalog", root_path, class: "text-link" %> · <%= link_to "View the source on GitHub", source_repository_url, class: "text-link" %></p>
</article>
